WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.980
Add interferon to human cells, and a set of genes switches on within minutes.

2
00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:11.740
Interferon is a protein that warns cells of a virus.

3
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.720
The genes still switch on when a drug stops the cells from making any new protein.

4
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:21.760
So every part of the path, from the cell surface to those genes, must already be in

5
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:22.760
the cell, waiting.

6
00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:24.260
What is the path made of?

7
00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:28.220
Interferon is a cytokine.

8
00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:32.940
A cytokine is a small secreted protein that tells other cells what to do.

9
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.980
Each chain of this kind of cytokine receptor crosses the membrane once, and it has

10
00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:43.520
no enzyme at all. Instead, its inside tail holds a separate tyrosine kinase.

11
00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:48.320
This kinase is called a JAK, and it is there before any cytokine arrives.

12
00:00:49.433 --> 00:00:50.953
Binding does one job.

13
00:00:51.473 --> 00:00:55.913
The cytokine sets receptor chains, and so their JAKs, in position against each

14
00:00:55.913 --> 00:00:58.613
other. In the simplest case there are two of each.

15
00:00:59.253 --> 00:01:02.153
Now the two JAKs are close enough to phosphorylate each other.

16
00:01:02.693 --> 00:01:06.473
Each adds a phosphate to its partner, and both become fully active.

17
00:01:07.153 --> 00:01:10.013
A receptor with no enzyme of its own has borrowed one.

18
00:01:12.017 --> 00:01:16.257
The active JAKs now phosphorylate tyrosines along the receptor tails.

19
00:01:17.037 --> 00:01:19.617
These phosphorylated tyrosines are docking sites.

20
00:01:20.177 --> 00:01:22.697
A protein called a STAT waits in the cytoplasm.

21
00:01:23.357 --> 00:01:28.097
It carries an SH2 domain, a module that binds a phosphate on a tyrosine.

22
00:01:28.757 --> 00:01:32.937
With that domain, the STAT docks on the receptor tail, next to the JAK.

23
00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:38.020
The JAK then adds one phosphate to the docked STAT, on a single tyrosine.

24
00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:41.420
Now the same SH2 domain has something new to hold.

25
00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:43.940
Many STATs already sit in loose pairs.

26
00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:49.220
Phosphorylation switches them into a pair held by this grip: each domain holds the

27
00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:51.100
partner's phosphorylated tyrosine.

28
00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:55.220
Two grips hold the pair, where one grip held the STAT to the receptor.

29
00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:57.760
So the same domain is used twice.

30
00:02:00.283 --> 00:02:04.703
The pair enters the nucleus, binds a specific DNA sequence, and switches on

31
00:02:04.703 --> 00:02:08.763
transcription. Count the proteins between the receptor complex and the gene.

32
00:02:09.243 --> 00:02:13.223
There is one kind, the Stat, with no second messenger and no chain of kinases.

33
00:02:14.103 --> 00:02:16.443
Interferon adds one partner, I R F nine.

34
00:02:17.023 --> 00:02:20.263
The Stats are already waiting, so genes answer within minutes.

35
00:02:20.883 --> 00:02:25.503
So keep this rule: a Stat, with one phosphate, carries the signal from receptor to

36
00:02:25.503 --> 00:02:25.763
gene.
